# redsand_v5.yml — RED SAND: FULL TANK, v5 (dialogue-bubble beats).
#
# The v5 build of the campaign. Same world, same cast, same three reversals —
# what is new is that a mission is a BEAT SCRIPT as well as four questions:
# arrive and the shortfall warning flashes, a commander steps between two
# technicians and speaks, a panel changes, the next stop opens.
#
# EVERY PLAYER-FACING LINE IN THIS FILE IS VERBATIM FROM THE BIBLE. The opening
# five sentences, the briefing card's seven lines, each stop's story setup,
# story-science connection, prompt, choices, verdict and wrong-path feedback,
# and every word of dialogue in every beat. Where a field the engine requires
# has no counterpart in the bible — a stop's `guide` and `takeaway` — it is
# written here and marked. Nothing the bible wrote has been reworded.
#
# Source: Mars_AP_Chemistry_Campaign_Implementation_Bible_v10.2_Compact_Glossary.md
#
# WHAT v7 CHANGED, and it is all about the four bars now being on screen:
#   - the opening card loses "A warning changes from amber to red: METHANE
#     SHORTFALL - 18.0%". v7: "Do not state the 18% shortfall separately; the
#     82% Flight-Ready Methane bar communicates it."
#   - the briefing card says "The Flight-Ready Methane bar is below target"
#     where v5 said "The methane tanks are 18% behind schedule".
#   - the arrival beat's world line opens the HUD at 82 / 88 / 72 / 70 instead
#     of flashing the shortfall, and its wall rows follow.
#   - the unit-path beat drops a shortfall row v7 does not ask for.
#   - the post-mission metric screen is new: it is `theme.metrics` in the
#     manifest and `engine/core/metricScreen.js`, not book content, because it
#     is arithmetic over the shift rather than words on a card.
#
# THIS BOOK IS ONE MISSION LONG. Mission 1 and the opening card, to get the beat
# machinery working before the other fourteen are written.
#
# Format is tools/BOOK_TEMPLATE.md; import with
#
#   node tools/import-book.mjs books/redsand_v5.yml redsand_v5 --verify

theme:
  id: redsand_v5
  title: 'Red Sand: Full Tank'
  subtitle: Propellant Lead · Arcadia Rise

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the areas
# Six areas, because six buildings on this site carry a case stand. The other
# eight places — the intake, the hydrogen store, the ice cut, the assay lab, the
# array shed, the battery bank, the tank farm and the pad office — are reached
# through `at:`, which sends the player to the object the question is about
# without making a fourteenth curriculum area out of it.
groups:
- id: GIBBS
  code: GIBBS
  name: Plant Control
  color: '#8d6bb5'
  desc: Where the ledgers are closed, the power is allocated and the plant is aimed.
  defaultLeader: abiola
  milestones:
  - Close the first ledger honestly
  - Find the one cause that fits every panel
  - Prove who did what, and when
  - Spend the last of everything on the right thing
- id: EQUIL
  code: EQUIL
  name: Reactor Hall
  color: '#c05a33'
  desc: What the Sabatier loop can give, what it gives out as heat, and what moves both.
  defaultLeader: herrera
  milestones:
  - Turn a schedule into a feed
  - Account for the heat as well as the product
  - State the balance the loop settles at
  - Certify a batch against the flight limits
- id: KINET
  code: KINET
  name: Catalyst Bay
  color: '#d19a2b'
  desc: How fast the reaction goes, what the catalyst does, and what takes it away.
  defaultLeader: sundqvist
  milestones:
  - Find which feed sets the ceiling
  - Get the rate law out of the data
  - Read the bed by where its heat is
  - Refuse the model with the best number
- id: PHASE
  code: PHASE
  name: Cold End
  color: '#4a86b8'
  desc: What condenses where, what sticks to what, and what must never reach a cold line.
  defaultLeader: cho
  milestones:
  - Rule out a path a substance cannot take
  - Keep water and carbon dioxide out of the cold end
  - Take a product away and move the balance
  - Say what is wrong with a tank that is full
- id: SOIL
  code: SOIL
  name: Water Plant
  color: '#9a8352'
  desc: Getting water out of frozen ground, measuring what is dissolved in it, and taking that out.
  defaultLeader: achebe
  milestones:
  - Tell concentration from amount
  - Measure against a standard rather than a wall meter
  - Treat what the next machine cannot survive
  - Test the certification on the newest sample
- id: ELEC
  code: ELEC
  name: Electrolysis Hall
  color: '#3f9e8c'
  desc: Splitting water with electricity, and what a finite number of kilowatt-hours buys.
  defaultLeader: demir
  milestones:
  - Name what happens at each electrode
  - Turn amps and hours into kilograms
  - Close hydrogen over the whole plant
  - Fund the chain that actually fills the tank

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ the cast
# Six speaking leads, one per area, each with something they want and something
# they cannot see. Six more who carry an observation or a consequence and never
# a subplot.
roster:
- id: abiola
  name: Laila Abiola
  role: Station Commander
  division: GIBBS
  bio: |
    Commands Arcadia Rise, which on any ordinary sol means arbitrating between
    six people who each own one number. Her question is always the same one:
    what can we defend? She would rather be handed a small honest figure than a
    reassuring one, and she says so on the first morning.

    She cancels work to make a point. A leak siren was sounding and two
    technicians were already walking valves down when she stopped them, because
    an intervention chosen before the ledger is read is a guess with a spanner
    in its hand.

    Her blind spot is agreement. Three panels showing the same number look to
    her like three witnesses, and it takes a dependency graph to show her they
    are one witness repeated.
  quiz:
  - q: Why does Abiola stop the valve work before any measurement has been argued about?
    a: Choosing an action first makes the evidence impossible to read afterwards
    wrong:
    - Sealing a valve on this station requires two signatures and only one of the two was available that morning
    - The leak siren has been unreliable below twelve bar since the plant was commissioned
    - Station policy forbids any physical intervention at all while a warning light is showing red
- id: nakata
  name: Suri Nakata
  role: Shift Controller
  division: GIBBS
  bio: |
    Keeps the load board, which means she is the person who knows what will go
    dark if a decision is taken carelessly. She writes every allocation down in
    pencil first and in ink only once somebody senior has said the sentence out
    loud.

    She has run this board through two dust seasons. What she contributes is
    memory: which circuits have been shed before, what came back slowly, and
    which one nobody has ever tried.
  quiz:
  - q: Why does Nakata write an allocation in pencil before anybody signs it?
    a: So it can still be argued with rather than quoted back
    wrong:
    - Ink is rationed on this station and every drop of it is reserved for flight paperwork
    - The load board is photographed at the end of every sol and pencil reproduces better
    - Pencil survives the cold in the control module considerably better than ink ever does
- id: sundqvist
  name: Ingrid Sundqvist
  role: Production and Catalyst Lead
  division: KINET
  bio: |
    Runs the plant against a calendar. Her question is how many kilograms by
    dawn, and she can answer it to two figures at any hour of any sol. Twice she
    has been right to push the bed harder when everybody else was being
    careful, and she has not forgotten either time.

    She reaches for rate first. If output is down, something is going too
    slowly, and the fix is more heat, more flow or more passes. That instinct is
    usually the fastest route to the answer and it is wrong whenever yield or
    purity is the thing that binds.

    She accuses people, and she takes it back in public when the data says to.
    Both are the same trait.
  quiz:
  - q: What makes Sundqvist's first instinct about a shortfall a real risk rather than a habit?
    a: Reaching for speed hides a limit on how much can be made at all
    wrong:
    - She has never before run a plant whose product had to be handed over as a certified liquid
    - She refuses on principle to read any figure she has not measured with her own instruments
    - Her production calendar was written months before anybody chose which catalyst charge to fly
- id: tanaka
  name: Rei Tanaka
  role: Maintenance Technician
  division: KINET
  bio: |
    Services the valves, the jackets and anything that has to be opened with a
    spanner. He is the one who knows that a blue stain near a methane valve is a
    service fluid he spilled two shifts ago, and he says so as soon as anybody
    asks him.

    His records are handwritten and complete. He is uneasy about that being
    treated as evidence, because a note proves what he wrote down and not what
    the pipe did.
  quiz:
  - q: Why is Tanaka uneasy when his maintenance note is offered as proof of a valve position?
    a: A note is what he wrote, not what the valve did
    wrong:
    - His handwriting has been misread by the control room on at least two previous occasions
    - Maintenance notes on this station are never countersigned by a second qualified technician
    - He was working alone in the bay that shift and cannot supply anybody as a witness
- id: herrera
  name: Tomás Herrera
  role: Reactor and Safety Engineer
  division: EQUIL
  bio: |
    Owns the Sabatier loop and the thermal envelope it has been tested inside.
    His question is what changed first, and he asks for raw timestamps before he
    will discuss anybody's conclusion. He thinks about the reactor as a balance
    rather than as a machine that goes fast or slow.

    What frightens him is a bed temperature reported as one number. The inlet
    and the outlet can disagree by fifty kelvin, and an average that stays
    respectable is exactly how a hot spot goes unseen until it is not a hot spot
    any more.

    His blind spot is secrecy. He held back an incomplete thermal record because
    he expected command to overreact to it, and that silence is most of why he
    spends three sols looking guilty.
  quiz:
  - q: Why does Herrera refuse to argue about a bed temperature given as a single figure?
    a: An average stays calm while one end runs over the limit
    wrong:
    - A thermowell will drift by as much as fifty kelvin over the life of one catalyst charge
    - The control system samples the outlet only once a minute and interpolates everything between
    - A single figure cannot be signed for under the flight rules this station operates to
- id: okafor
  name: Amaka Okafor
  role: Reactor Operator
  division: EQUIL
  bio: |
    Stands the reactor watch, which means she is the person who actually turns
    the set point when somebody senior decides it should move. She keeps her own
    log of what she was asked to do and what the loop did afterwards.

    She is careful about the difference between the two. A set point is an
    instruction and a temperature is a measurement, and her log has separate
    columns for them because she has seen those confused.
  quiz:
  - q: Why does Okafor keep set points and measured temperatures in separate columns?
    a: One is an instruction and the other is a measurement
    wrong:
    - The two are read from instruments that sit on entirely different calibration schedules
    - Flight rules require every instruction to be logged well apart from any measured data
    - Set points are recorded in degrees Celsius on this station and temperatures in kelvin
- id: cho
  name: Mei-Ling Cho
  role: Water and Cryogenics Engineer
  division: PHASE
  bio: |
    Owns everything that gets dangerously cold, which is why her question is
    what can physically travel there. She will not accept a residue sample with
    a broken chain of custody, and she said so to a production lead in front of
    four people.

    She thinks in properties. A molecule's shape decides whether its charges
    cancel, and what is left over decides whether the substance rides a dry gas
    stream or condenses out of it a hundred metres upstream. That reasoning has
    saved a cold line twice.

    Her blind spot is tidiness. She trusts a separation model further than she
    trusts a maintenance history, and the messy record is sometimes the true
    one.
  quiz:
  - q: What is Cho actually testing when she asks what could physically travel to a spot?
    a: Whether the substance's own properties allow that route at all
    wrong:
    - Whether anybody at all was rostered to walk that section of pipe during the shift in question
    - Whether the line was warm enough along its whole length to keep a gas from liquefying inside it
    - Whether the sample bag was properly sealed before it was carried away from the suspect valve
- id: vasquez
  name: Elena Vasquez
  role: Pad Controller
  division: PHASE
  bio: |
    Runs the loading checklist at the pad office, thirty metres from the
    vehicle. Her job is a sequence, and her contribution to any argument is what
    the sequence will not allow: a transfer that cannot be reversed once the
    umbilical is warm.

    She has stopped two loadings. Neither was popular and both were on the
    checklist, which is the only defence she has ever needed.
  quiz:
  - q: Why does Vasquez treat a loading as different from most plant operations?
    a: It cannot be undone once the umbilical is warm
    wrong:
    - The pad office has no direct voice line to the reactor hall and never has had one
    - Loading is the only operation on this station that runs on battery power alone
    - The loading checklist is written by the vehicle manufacturer rather than by the station
- id: achebe
  name: Rosalind Achebe
  role: Analytical and Electrochemistry Lead
  division: SOIL
  bio: |
    Wants every important number traceable to something physical. Her question
    is against which standard, and she carries a sealed one rather than
    calibrating from yesterday's plant water. She is the reason a launch does
    not go on a green light nobody can trace.

    Her instinct is to break a chain of assumption by taking a fresh sample from
    a different place with a different reference. It is slow, it costs a rover
    trip, and twice in this fortnight it is the only thing that finds the
    answer.

    Her blind spot is the clock. She will ask for perfect evidence when
    sufficient evidence was available an hour ago, and people have started
    deciding without her because of it.
  quiz:
  - q: Why does Achebe refuse to calibrate an instrument from the plant's own recent water?
    a: A reference from inside the system cannot expose the system's own bias
    wrong:
    - Plant water changes its composition considerably faster than any sealed reference does
    - The wall meters are read to far fewer significant figures than her own portable instrument
    - Recent samples have not yet cleared the polishing columns and are not representative
- id: moreau
  name: Denis Moreau
  role: Rover Operator
  division: SOIL
  bio: |
    Drives the long haul out to the ice cut and back, which is three hundred
    metres of graded track and the only part of this station that feels like a
    planet. He takes the field blanks because somebody has to hold a clean
    bottle open in the open air and then seal it again.

    He is precise about where a sample came from. A vial with the wrong cut face
    written on it is worse than no vial, and he has thrown two away for that.
  quiz:
  - q: Why does Moreau discard a sample whose cut face was recorded wrongly?
    a: A sample with no place attached answers nothing
    wrong:
    - A vial that has been relabelled by hand will no longer seal to flight standard
    - The rover logs its positions automatically and the two records are required to match
    - Ice taken from different faces of the cut melts at measurably different rates
- id: demir
  name: Yusuf Demir
  role: Power and Life-Support Officer
  division: ELEC
  bio: |
    Holds the electrical budget and the habitat's heat, air and water, which are
    the same budget seen from two ends. His question is what stops if we do
    that, and he asks it every time somebody proposes running the stacks harder.

    He opened the habitat reserve breaker log during a reactor meeting to make
    the point that the plant is not the only load on the plain. Six people are
    the other one, and nobody is allowed to shed them.

    His blind spot is scarcity itself. He frames every choice as something being
    taken away, and he underrates how much a recovered recycle loop can put back
    into the same budget.
  quiz:
  - q: Why does Demir open the habitat breaker log during an argument about reactor output?
    a: To make the crew's heat and air visible as a competing load
    wrong:
    - Because the breaker log is the only surviving record of what the array really delivered
    - Because reactor meetings are where every power allocation on this station is formally signed
    - Because the habitat and the reactor are wired through one shared set of heavy cables
- id: bhatt
  name: Priya Bhatt
  role: Habitat Medic
  division: ELEC
  bio: |
    Looks after six people, which on this station mostly means looking after
    temperature, oxygen partial pressure and sleep. She puts the night heat load
    on the board in numbers when an argument about power starts to sound
    abstract.

    She does not argue about chemistry. What she supplies is the consequence:
    what happens in the habitat, and how quickly, if a circuit that looks
    discretionary is shed.
  quiz:
  - q: Why does Bhatt put the habitat's night heat load on the board as a number?
    a: An abstract argument hides what the crew actually loses
    wrong:
    - Because the medical log is required to record every power allocation decision taken
    - Because heat is the only load she is formally authorised to speak about at a meeting
    - Because the night heat load is the largest single electrical draw anywhere on the plain

glossary:
# THE BIBLE'S OWN ENTRIES, VERBATIM, AND NOTHING ELSE.
#
# v10.2 moved the glossary out of one campaign-wide appendix and onto the mission
# card that first needs each term — "Worth knowing first · Glossary terms", written
# as compact one-line `Term: definition` pairs, with the instruction that the
# implementation "should lift those authored entries directly rather than expanding
# them into alias or definition subfields". So the `def` below is the bible's line
# for that term, unedited, and `aliases` is the only thing added: it is not copy,
# it is the list of spellings `checkJargon` matches the prose against.
#
# The previous build carried a sixty-entry appendix written for all fifteen
# missions, four of whose entries were flagged in books/redsand_v5.BIBLE_GAPS.md as
# written here because the source defined nothing. v10.2 answers all four — it
# defines electron, mixture, kinetic energy and the rest on the cards that use them
# — so the flags are gone with the entries they were attached to. Twenty-five terms,
# from missions 1, 2 and 3, which is exactly what this book ships.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- mission 1
- name: Particle
  aliases: [particle, particles]
  def: One counted piece of matter. In this mission, a particle may be an atom, a molecule, or an ion.
- name: Electric charge
  aliases: [electric charge, charge, charged, net charge, positive charge, negative charge]
  def: A property that can be positive or negative and makes particles attract or repel. An object with equal positive and negative charge is neutral.
- name: Electron
  aliases: [electron, electrons]
  def: A particle with negative electric charge found in atoms. Electron arrangement helps determine how atoms join and whether a particle has a net charge.
- name: Chemical bond
  aliases: [chemical bond, chemical bonds, bond, bonds, bonded]
  def: A strong connection that holds atoms together inside a molecule. Breaking or making bonds changes how atoms are grouped but does not create or destroy the atoms.
- name: Chemical reaction
  aliases: [chemical reaction, reaction, reactions, reacts]
  def: Rearranges atoms by breaking or making chemical bonds. The kinds and counts of atoms remain the same before and after the change.
- name: Atom
  aliases: [atom, atoms]
  def: The smallest ordinary piece of one kind of matter that keeps that identity. Counting atoms lets the plant check whether matter has disappeared.
- name: Molecule
  aliases: [molecule, molecules]
  def: Two or more atoms joined by chemical bonds. Methane and carbon dioxide are molecules, so one molecule contains several atoms.
- name: Ion
  aliases: [ion, ions]
  def: An atom or group of atoms with an electrical charge. It is not the same thing as a neutral atom or molecule.
- name: Mole
  aliases: [mole, moles, kilomole, kilomoles, kmol]
  def: A fixed count of particles, the same count for every substance. One mole contains 6.022 x 10^23 particles; one kilomole contains one thousand moles.
- name: Molar mass
  aliases: [molar mass]
  def: The mass of one mole of a substance. It connects a mass on a scale to the number of particles in the plant.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- mission 2
- name: Sabatier reaction
  aliases: [sabatier reaction, sabatier]
  def: Combines carbon dioxide and hydrogen to make methane and water. It is the reaction Arcadia Rise uses to make the crew's fuel.
- name: Reactant
  aliases: [reactant, reactants]
  def: A starting substance used by a chemical reaction. Carbon dioxide and hydrogen are the two reactants in the plant's methane reactor.
- name: Product
  aliases: [product, products]
  def: A substance made by a chemical reaction. Methane and water are the products of the Sabatier reaction.
- name: Coefficient
  aliases: [coefficient, coefficients]
  def: The number written before a chemical formula in a balanced equation. Coefficients compare particle counts and mole amounts, not masses.
- name: Limiting reactant
  aliases: [limiting reactant, limiting reagent, limiting, limits]
  def: The starting substance that runs out first. It sets the greatest amount of product the reactor can make.
- name: Excess reactant
  aliases: [excess reactant, excess]
  def: A starting substance left after the limiting reactant runs out. Its presence does not mean the reaction can continue.
- name: Theoretical yield
  aliases: [theoretical yield]
  def: The greatest product amount allowed by the measured reactants and balanced equation. It is a ceiling, not a promise that the plant reaches it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- mission 3
- name: Pressure
  aliases: [pressure, pressures]
  def: Force spread over an area, caused here by gas particles striking the tank walls. Total pressure can stay high even when the wrong gas is inside.
- name: Volume
  aliases: [volume]
  def: The amount of space occupied by the gas. A sealed tank gives the gas a fixed space unless the hardware changes.
- name: Absolute temperature
  aliases: [absolute temperature, kelvin]
  def: Measures thermal motion from the lowest possible temperature. Gas-law calculations use kelvin rather than degrees Celsius.
- name: Kinetic energy
  aliases: [kinetic energy, kinetic, average kinetic energy]
  def: Energy an object has because it moves. At the same temperature, different gases have the same average kinetic energy even though lighter particles move faster.
- name: Mixture
  aliases: [mixture, mixtures, mixed, mixes]
  def: Contains more than one substance without joining them into a new substance. Each gas in a mixture contributes part of the total pressure.
- name: Composition
  aliases: [composition]
  def: States which substances are present and how much of each one the mixture contains. It can change even while total pressure stays the same.
- name: Partial pressure
  aliases: [partial pressure, partial pressures]
  def: One gas's share of the total pressure. It measures how much that gas contributes even when the gauge shows only the total.
- name: Mole fraction
  aliases: [mole fraction]
  def: The part of all gas particles belonging to one gas. A value of 0.68 means 68 out of every 100 gas particles are that gas.

# ------------------------------------------------- what the bible does not define
# THREE ENTRIES THE SOURCE OWES, and the gates name all three. v10.2's own rule is
# that "every player-facing technical term resolves through the glossary without
# depending on an undefined word" — and its cards then use these three without an
# entry. They are written here to keep the build moving, flagged in
# books/redsand_v5.BIBLE_GAPS.md, and should be replaced by the bible's wording.
#
# Two of the three are lifted rather than invented: `Ideal gas law` is mission 3's
# own equation block — the equation, what it is for, and every symbol it names —
# and `Methane` is mission 2's Sabatier entry said the other way round. Only
# `Stoichiometric` is written from nothing, because the word appears in a verdict
# the bible wrote and in no entry the bible wrote.
- name: Methane
  aliases: [methane, ch4]
  def: The fuel this plant is built to make, written CH4. One methane molecule is one carbon atom joined to four hydrogen atoms.
- name: Stoichiometric
  aliases: [stoichiometric, stoichiometry]
  def: To do with the mole amounts a balanced equation compares. A stoichiometric calculation converts one substance's moles into another's through the coefficients.
- name: Ideal gas law
  aliases: [ideal gas law, pv = nrt, pv, nrt]
  def: Finds the total amount of gas from pressure, volume, and temperature, written PV = nRT. P is pressure, V is volume, n is moles of gas, R is the gas constant, and T is absolute temperature in kelvin.

# --------------------------------------------------------------- the campaign
#
# One mission, four stops, five beats. Every line of copy below is the bible's.
#
# WHERE THE STOPS STAND. The bible puts all four in Plant Control and calls that
# "one location". They are, and `at:` is what delivers it: each stop names a
# fixture in Plant Control and the player is sent there. What differs is the
# *area* each stop belongs to, which is a curriculum bucket rather than a place
# — four stops in one area would make three of them person stops, and the bible
# is explicit that stops 2, 3 and 4 are answered at a board and a ledger desk.
# Rooms a beat may fire in that are not curriculum areas. See `beatRooms` in
# tools/import-book.mjs: these are the places the campaign's missions happen at,
# authored in themes/redsand_v5/minors.js, which the importer cannot see.
beatRooms: [INTAKE, HSTORE, TANKS, PAD, ASSAY, CUT, HAB, GARAGE, ARRAY, BATT, SHOP]

missions:
# 15 mission fragment(s), assembled by tools/assemble-book.mjs from
# redsand_v5/ — edit the fragments, not this file.
# ============================================================ MISSION 1
# Lifted from ../fpl_gpt/Mars_AP_Chemistry_Campaign_Implementation_Bible_v10.4_House_Style.md by tools/v10extract.mjs. Every quoted string below is
# the bible's, verbatim; engine/dev/bibleParity.mjs holds it to that.
- title: "The Shortfall"
  card:
    header: "15 SHIFTS UNTIL THE LAUNCH WINDOW CLOSES"
    title: "THE SHORTFALL"
    goNow: "Go to Plant Control and meet Commander Laila Abiola, the mission commander, at the carbon ledger."
    body: "The ascent vehicle still lacks the methane fuel needed to carry the crew home. The crew suspects a leak, but closing the wrong valves could waste a shift and reduce production. At Plant Control, classify samples, convert tank mass into moles, and account for the carbon entering and leaving the plant. By the end of the mission, decide whether a large methane leak can explain the shortage."
    objective: "Use the production records to decide whether a large methane leak can explain the fuel shortage."
  objective: "Use the production records to decide whether a large methane leak can explain the fuel shortage."
  stake: "The ascent vehicle still lacks the methane fuel needed to carry the crew home. The crew suspects a leak, but closing the wrong valves could waste a shift and reduce production. At Plant Control, classify samples, convert tank mass into moles, and account for the carbon entering and leaving the plant. By the end of the mission, decide whether a large methane leak can explain the shortage."
  takeaway: "A nearly closed carbon ledger weakens a large methane-leak explanation; it does not identify the real cause."
  segue: "Mission decision: A large methane leak cannot explain the fuel gap. The new count finds 99.8% of the carbon. Work on random valves stops. The plant still needs fuel. The next test asks if the air intake brings in enough carbon dioxide."
  # The mission card's own "Primer concepts", printed on the plan card.
  primer:
  - "Matter can change form during a reaction, but atoms are not created or destroyed."
  - "A mass shortage is not automatically a leak; first ask whether the unit conversion and atom count close."
  - "Chemical formulas show how many atoms of each element are present in one particle."

  # The bible's beat script. `world` is its World state line, `panel` its
  # Panel/HUD text, `bubbles` its dialogue. `on:` and any `stage:` rows are
  # the implementation's: which trigger, and which board the change lands on.
  beats:
  - id: arrival
    # Arrival | Plant Control | automatic when the player enters
    on: { enter: true, at: GIBBS }
    world: "The four-bar HUD opens at METHANE 82%, OXYGEN 88%, POWER"
    # unlocks: Stop 1 at the sample tray.
    bubbles:
    - who: abiola   # Abiola
      say: "Those valves stay open until we know whether matter is actually missing. We have fifteen shifts to make the fuel that gets us home. Start with the ledger."
  - id: after-stop-1
